{
  "abstract": "Paper 93 built Diophantine septuples over the function field of an elliptic curve E from a constant a and six translates of h = (u² − 1)/a, deg u = 2. Paper 95 classified them when the edges use options (i), (ii) and 2-torsion shifts, except for type P4 (since excluded by Paper 97) and three systems of type P1 (a coset of Z/6) with a twisted edge at the 2-torsion class 3g. We prove the three systems empty, exactly over Q, through subsystems whose generators are among theirs (inclusion asserted in the scripts). In chart0 the option-(i) edges at g and 2g alone leave four points with c = (−2 ± i)/5, where the twisted edge fails. In the main chart, option (ii) at 2g fills the four points where h² = −1 with the halves of −2g, while a twisted edge at 3g needs a half of ±3g there, so 3g = ±2g (impossible). Hence, under the hypotheses of Paper 95's Theorem C, every such septuple lies on one of Paper 93's two components, and none is defined over a real field; one input is msolve's multimodular computation, as in Paper 95. We also explain the √−1. For a source with u(P + T_e) = κ/u(P) and option-(i) edges at S₁, S₂, the pinning law t(2P) = t(S₁ + S₂) + t(S₁ − S₂) holds, with t = 1/(x − e) and P the pole of u (hand proof plus one exact identity). When S₁ − S₂ ∈ E[2], the law makes −1 a square by an explicit formula. In P1, g − (−2g) = 3g. The same corollary removes, for real and rational septuples, the 12 one-prime systems of type Z/2² × Z in Paper 95's Proposition 7.6. No rational septuple and no nonexistence proof follow.",
